WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.498
A dependable saving routine is part of lesson design.

2
00:00:03.698 --> 00:00:09.118
An editable project keeps the objects, records or timeline available for revision.

3
00:00:09.318 --> 00:00:13.297
A shareable export gives someone a view or finished product.

4
00:00:13.497 --> 00:00:15.501
They serve different purposes.

5
00:00:15.701 --> 00:00:21.063
Before class, save a small project and export it in a format the app supports.

6
00:00:21.263 --> 00:00:24.605
Reopen the editable file and inspect the export.

7
00:00:24.805 --> 00:00:29.129
That quick test is more useful than assuming that the download succeeded.

8
00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:33.456
Browser drafts are tied to the device, browser and site origin.

9
00:00:33.656 --> 00:00:39.507
A draft on DrawSplat dot org is not automatically the same draft on SplatWorks dot org.

10
00:00:39.707 --> 00:00:43.548
Clearing browser data can remove local recovery information.

11
00:00:43.748 --> 00:00:50.980
Google Drive connections and district whiteboard accounts also have different roles; they do not make every app a

12
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.043
shared cloud document.

13
00:00:53.243 --> 00:01:00.218
Follow the app guide, check the saved status and use exported files when moving work between contexts.

14
00:01:00.418 --> 00:01:03.493
Use anonymous practice content while you learn.

15
00:01:03.693 --> 00:01:08.158
Save a file with the course name, activity and version in the filename.

16
00:01:08.358 --> 00:01:13.153
Close the project, reopen the copy and check one important detail.

17
00:01:13.353 --> 00:01:16.867
Record where you will store the source and finished export.

18
00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:20.904
Finally, write a one-sentence fallback for the learning task.

19
00:01:21.104 --> 00:01:28.619
If a device or connection fails, students can still predict, examine evidence and revise a claim on paper.

